# name: test/sql/f1_hier_negbin.test
# description: F1 hierarchical count GLM, exercised as the C-parts safety-stock agent would use it
# group: [sql]

require anofox_bayes

#######################################
# Setup: a spare-parts demand history
#######################################

# Weekly issues of fourteen spare parts from one plant's store, one row per part per
# week. Six are running bearings with more than nine months of history; eight are
# seals introduced last month, with five weeks each. That mixture is the point: a
# C-parts catalogue is mostly the second kind, and a per-part model fitted
# independently on five observations produces an interval so wide it is useless and a
# point estimate so noisy it is worse.
#
# The table was drawn from the model itself -- true rate ~ lognormal around six units
# a week with a pooling scale of 0.7, and a dispersion of 2.5 on top -- so the answers
# below can be checked against a truth. It is literal rather than generated so that
# the same numbers feed the Rust suite and this file, and the two cannot drift.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE issues(part VARCHAR, week INTEGER, units INTEGER);

